WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:04.300
-Our first guest hosts the Emmy-
and Peabody Award-winning show

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.634
"Last Week Tonight,"
which airs Sundays on HBO

00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:07.968
and streams on MAX.

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:09.167
Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend

00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:10.767
John Oliver, everybody.

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:13.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:19.701
♪♪

00:00:19.701 --> 00:00:25.667
♪♪

00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:28.267
Yes.

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:30.901
You know, it was a --
it was a bad time.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:34.601
The strike was a bad time.
-Yeah, yeah. Really bad.

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.534
-We got to spend some time
together.

00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:40.067
-Let's throw - - I know cursing
is --

00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:41.901
-Encouraged.
-Oh, sure.

00:00:41.901 --> 00:00:43.667
Let's call it a [bleep] bad time

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.267
because otherwise,
what are intensifiers for?

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:47.601
-Yeah.
-Yes. Very bad.

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:49.901
-And, you know, we did
spend some time together

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:51.067
during the strike.
That was nice.

00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:52.634
But we were talking
about a lot of people

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:55.634
just assume when you're not
working that is a positive.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:57.300
-Yeah, they sure do assume
that, don't they?

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.567
-Yeah.
-They assume that

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:01.033
almost every day, and they
assume that reminding you

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:03.434
of that might be a pleasant
thing for you to hear.

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:05.834
Yeah. It was nonstop.
People would say,

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:07.133
"Well, at least
you're getting some time off.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.701
It must feel like a vacation."

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:13.534
And I most of the time
did not respond to that.

00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:15.133
And when I did,
I was not as gracious

00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:17.534
as I might otherwise have been.

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:19.100
I was on an elevator
with my wife,

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.834
and someone got off saying,
"Yeah, well, at least

00:01:21.834 --> 00:01:23.400
you're getting a nice vacation.
That must be nice."

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.267
And I was following
them off to say,

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:30.501
"You're [bleep] wrong
about that,"

00:01:30.501 --> 00:01:32.767
until my wife pulled me back,
and said, it's not our floor.

00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:35.100
I know it's not our floor.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.467
But that's -- that's not
appreciating the difficulty

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:40.501
of the situation that
I'm in right now.

00:01:40.501 --> 00:01:42.534
-Right before the strike,
you were here,

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:43.601
and it was lovely
to have you here,

00:01:43.601 --> 00:01:44.968
and your parents
were in the audience.

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:46.100
-Yes, that's true.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.000
-Although you stressed to us --
-I did.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.801
-Don't mention it and don't cut
to them.

00:01:50.801 --> 00:01:52.434
-That's right.

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:54.968
Your producers asked, would they
like a camera on them?

00:01:54.968 --> 00:01:58.434
And I had to say,
I cannot overstate enough

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:01.400
just how little they want
a camera on them right here.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.167
It would be the meanest thing
I could possibly do to them.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:07.167
And I've not been
a perfect child.

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:08.300
But yeah, they really did.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.167
They were so excited
to be here, though,

00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:13.601
and the thing they said
afterwards was it was really,

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:16.834
really fun to go
and watch a real TV show.

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:26.300
And they had been, Seth,
at my show the previous night.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.601
Gets one step worse because I
went, "Oh, I think I understand

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:31.133
what you mean,"
because, you know,

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:32.567
it's like it's -- "30 Rock"

00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.267
is kind of a really
exciting place to be.

00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:36.701
So I think I see it.
And they said, "Well,

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:39.467
could we go and see Colbert
next time that we're here?"

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:40.667
And I said, "Why?"
And they said,

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:43.100
"Because it would be nice
to see a real guest."

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.434
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.434 --> 00:02:49.133
Wow. Yeah.

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:52.367
So if there's any sense
of why I'm like this,

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.667
there's a little snapshot.

00:02:54.667 --> 00:02:56.834
That's why my feet are --
-You kind of feel like --

00:02:56.834 --> 00:02:58.801
there should be
a Huffington Post article

00:02:58.801 --> 00:03:03.234
that's like, "John Oliver's
parents destroy John Oliver."

00:03:03.234 --> 00:03:06.467
-Eviscerate their own boy.

00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:08.534
-A very well structured
two-part burn on you.

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.200
-Yeah. That's right. Yeah.
-Also that thing of --

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.000
I mean, by the way,
don't blame us for asking like,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.334
do you want a camera
on your parents?

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:15.868
Because, you know, of course,
when my parents come,

00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:18.968
they actually bring a camera,

00:03:18.968 --> 00:03:20.701
just in case
something's wrong with ours.

00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:23.100
-Your dad is permanently live
streaming himself,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.434
just so you've got two cameras,

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:26.934
so you can edit around him.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:30.801
-Now we also talked, you know,
people like us, hosts,

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:34.234
we get asked -- it makes
natural sense to people,

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:35.968
"Oh, you should host
my charity event."

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:37.834
-Oh, yeah.
-And you, over the years,

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:40.367
have just eliminated
multiple charities

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.067
where you're suitable for.

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:43.801
-Yeah, I talk a lot
of [bleep].

00:03:43.801 --> 00:03:47.234
-Yeah.
-So helpfully for me that --

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:49.234
Yeah, I think I --
-It's, like, narrowed the field.

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:51.801
-Exactly. There's not many --
and so there's not many

00:03:51.801 --> 00:03:55.534
that I can do
or that I'm welcome at now.

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:57.100
-But then you mentioned the one
thing.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.367
-I mentioned last time.

00:03:58.367 --> 00:03:59.934
"Sesame Street's" the greatest.
I love --

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:01.200
-Of course, the best.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.634
-Obviously, I love them
in general so much.

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:04.667
I love the idea of them getting
as much money as they can.

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:07.567
So every year,
I do the "Sesame Street" gala.

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:09.601
So I mentioned it here
because it's so fun,

00:04:09.601 --> 00:04:12.067
and I thought
this would be nice, too,

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:16.667
and I knew it was coming up.
I was then booed at that gala.

00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:20.734
And I want you to think
about how hard it is

00:04:20.734 --> 00:04:21.968
to make that happen.

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:24.968
The theme of the evening
was kindness.

00:04:24.968 --> 00:04:27.200
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.234
That's not easy to do.
Just to explain what happened --

00:04:30.234 --> 00:04:32.767
So obviously, the whole point is
raise as much money as they can.

00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:35.200
They got a big sponsor.
They had Bank of America

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.667
were going to be their sponsor,

00:04:36.667 --> 00:04:37.901
and I think they got
Bank of America

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:39.300
by making up this nonsense award

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.834
they were going to give
to one of the executives.

00:04:40.834 --> 00:04:45.133
I was going to go on after her.
And she walked on and she said,

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.567
"I've been thinking about
how appropriate it is

00:04:47.567 --> 00:04:50.400
that Bank of America and "Sesame
Street" have teamed up tonight,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.934
because we are two organizations
built on kindness."

00:04:53.934 --> 00:04:57.434
And I was standing
at the side of the stage.

00:04:57.434 --> 00:05:00.467
I remember thinking,
"Hm.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:04.534
I'm going to let that go,
because no one needs me

00:05:04.534 --> 00:05:07.634
to create a scene."
Alright now, you know,

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:08.901
she's out.
All the characters

00:05:08.901 --> 00:05:10.400
are out there with her.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.868
The whole point
is that we raise money.

00:05:11.868 --> 00:05:14.801
No one needs an incident
just because she said something

00:05:14.801 --> 00:05:18.634
that I personally find
a little odd to say,

00:05:18.634 --> 00:05:21.267
so I can let that go.
I will choose to let that go.

00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:22.801
I'm going to make
the better choice right now.

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:26.067
Then she said it three more
times in the next five minutes,

00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:28.801
and I snapped and I walked out
and said,

00:05:28.801 --> 00:05:31.601
"It's been odd to hear talk of
kindness from Bank of America,

00:05:31.601 --> 00:05:33.434
an institution that had
the highest foreclosure rates

00:05:33.434 --> 00:05:35.434
over the last ten years."

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:37.567
Boos bouncing --

00:05:37.567 --> 00:05:42.300
Oh, that's the opposite
of the reaction in that room.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.734
And the worst thing, the thing
that has imprinted itself

00:05:44.734 --> 00:05:49.534
on my memory, is all I remember
is Bert looking up at me

00:05:49.534 --> 00:05:52.567
with such disappointment
in his eyes.

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:55.501
Just, "Why are you like this?

00:05:55.501 --> 00:06:00.634
You're behaving erratically,
and I live with Ernie."

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:03.067
It wasn't great, so I would
like to do it again.

00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:06.634
"Sesame Street," have me back.
I'll be a good boy this time.

00:06:06.634 --> 00:06:08.334
-Yeah.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:09.501
They can trust you on that.

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:12.667
-Oh, definitely.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:15.601
Just have McKinsey be a sponsor.
They'll be fine.

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:16.801
-I've got a lot more
to ask you.

00:06:16.801 --> 00:06:18.968
We'll be right back with more
from John Oliver.

